.TH BOOK 1
.SH NAME
book, book2tex, mkindex \- make a book
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B book
[
.B -f
] [
.B -l
] [
.BI -m " macros"
] [
.BI -p " works.bib"
] [
.BI -S " style"
] [
.BI -o " book.pdf"
]
.I book.ms
.br
.B book2tex
[
.BI -c " class\fR[\fP,options\fR]\fP"
] [
.BI -m " macros"
] [
.BI -S " style"
] [
.BI -p " works.bib"
] [
.BI -o " book.tex"
] [
.I book.ms
]
.br
.B mkindex
[
.B -l
] [
.I book.out ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Book
makes a PDF of a book written with
.BR -mbook ,
the macros of
.IR troff (6),
or with a class built on them, named by
.BR -m :
.B article
.RB ( -marticle ,
a journal's article in two columns)
or
.B memo
.RB ( -mmemo ,
a technical memorandum or report).
It runs
.IR refer (1)
with the database
.I works.bib
(its citations in the style,
.B chicago
by default, with its options:
.B "-S chicago,british,nodoi"
as a British thesis has them),
.IR pic (1),
.IR chess (1),
.IR tbl (1)
and
.IR eqn (1)
(each if the book has anything for it),
.IR troff (1)
as often as the book's table of contents, references and index need
to settle (each pass learning from the one before, through
.IR book .aux),
and
.IR tr2pdf (1).
Troff's output is kept as
.IR book .out,
for the document views.
.PP
The PDF is tagged, for those who hear it read or reflow it: its
headings, paragraphs, quotations, notes, contents, equations (each
described in words), tables, pictures, bibliography and index are its
structure, read in order; its running heads and folios are not.
It claims PDF/UA-1, unless a picture was left with nothing to describe
it
.RB ( .alt
in
.IR troff (6)):
then
.I book
says so, and the PDF is tagged but makes no claim.
A book with
.B .printindex
has its index made by
.I mkindex
after each pass.
.PP
A book is set as a draft unless
.B -f
is given: then as it goes to press, with microtypography (punctuation
hanging into the margins, glyphs widened or narrowed a little), as
.IR troff 's
.B .mtp
and
.B .mte
set them.
.PP
With
.BR -l ,
.I book
sets the book to be looked at as it is written: troff's output to
standard output, no PDF, no index made. Xenith's document view runs it so, and with
Xenith's
.B Live
sets a book, article or memorandum again a moment after each edit
(see EXAMPLES).
.PP
.I Book2tex
writes the book, article or memorandum as LaTeX, for a publisher or a
journal who wants it: each of the macros as the LaTeX it stands for,
the equations as LaTeX's mathematics, the tables as tabulars (their
horizontal rules
.IR booktabs ',
their vertical ones only if the table asks for them, as
.IR tbl (1)
draws them), the citations as the class's, the pictures and chess
boards (with
.BR -o )
drawn by
.IR pic (1)
or
.IR chess (1),
troff and tr2pdf as PDFs of their own beside it, each the size of
its ink, its labels' equations set by eqn.
The class is that of the source's macros
.RB ( -m ,
or the line near its top that says how it is set):
LaTeX's
.B book
for
.BR -mbook ,
its
.B article
for
.B -marticle
(in two columns, or one) and
.B -mmemo
(the memorandum's head as troff sets it), each for LuaLaTeX in
Libertinus, with biblatex and biber, the citations in the style
.B -S
names as
.IR refer 's
are:
.B chicago
(the Chicago Manual's notes, biblatex-chicago's; its options
.B nodoi
and
.BR nourl ),
.B numeric
(numbered, in the order cited) or
.B authordate
(biblatex-chicago's, which refer has not).
.TP
.BI "-c ieee" "\fR[\fP,conference\fR]\fP"
A paper for the IEEE: IEEEtran's journal (or conference), for
pdfLaTeX; the authors as its notes or blocks have them, the abstract,
the keywords as its index terms, a table's caption above it, the
citations BibTeX's in IEEEtran's style
.RB ( "\ecite{key}" ,
.B "\ecite[p.~5]{key}"
for a place in the work, before the sentence's stop).
.TP
.BI "-c acm" "\fR[\fP,format\fR]\fP"
A paper for the ACM: acmart's
.B acmsmall
(or
.BR sigconf ,
.B acmlarge
and its other formats), for pdfLaTeX; each author's institution, city
and country from
.B .affil
(its last part after a comma the country, the one before it the city:
.BR ".affil \(dqBell Laboratories, Murray Hill, USA\(dq" ;
with no country it is written
.B [country]
and said), the abstract and keywords before
.BR \emaketitle ,
each figure's
.B .alt
as its
.BR \eDescription ,
the citations BibTeX's in ACM's style.
The rights, the venue and the CCS concepts are the ACM's to give: the
LaTeX says where they go.
.PP
For pdfLaTeX, a character inputenc knows (accented Latin letters,
quotation marks, dashes) is kept as it is; a mathematical one (Greek,
arrows, relations, the logical and set-theoretic symbols, ℝ) is
LaTeX's command for it, in the text or in an equation alike
.RB ( \eensuremath ,
or
.B \eTextOrMath
where the text has its own); any other is said, and written
.BR [U+ XXXX ].
A request or macro with no LaTeX of its own is kept as a comment, and
said.
.PP
.I Mkindex
reads troff's output for the entries
.B .index
put there and each page's folio, and writes the index
.B .printindex
sets, in the order of
.IR "The Chicago Manual of Style" .
With
.B -l
each page is a link to it.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
book -p works.bib thesis.ms
book -m article -S numeric -p works.bib paper.ms
book -m memo memo.ms
book -f -p works.bib -o press.pdf thesis.ms
book2tex -p works.bib -o thesis.tex thesis.ms
book2tex -c ieee -p works.bib -o paper.tex paper.ms
book2tex -c acm,sigconf -p works.bib -o paper.tex paper.ms
.EE
.PP
A paper for the IEEE or the ACM is then set as their systems set it:
.EX
pdflatex paper; bibtex paper; pdflatex paper; pdflatex paper
.EE
.PP
A line near the top of a file says how it is set, for Xenith's
document view, which sets it with
.BR "book -l" ,
as it is edited when
.B Live
is executed in the window's tag:
.EX
\&.\e" book -m article -S numeric -p works.bib
.EE
Its words after
.B book
are
.IR book 's
options
.RB ( -f ,
.BR -m ,
.BR -p ,
.BR -S );
the file's name may follow them, as in a line showing how it is made.
A file with no such line that uses
.BR -mbook 's
headings or paragraphs
.RB ( .chapter ,
.BR .section ,
.BR .part ,
.BR .maketitle ,
.BR .p )
is set by
.BR "book -l" ;
any other by
.I troff
with the macros its name gives.
Its cross-references are those its
.B book.aux
holds the first time (a chapter of a book made whole before), then
those of the last time it was set.
